e logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07d810ce-010c-5a07-8174-f01d9b807a60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110487 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡7+3{log}_{5}⁡3 & =3({log}_{5}⁡7+{log}_{5}⁡3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110488 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110488 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\ln\\left(x^2 y\\right)$ as a sum of two logarithms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "679a8e3b-ef6c-565d-ab37-613dedfdf3df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110488 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110489 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110489 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\log_2(3)=k$ and $\\log_2(x)=p,$ what is the value of $\\log_2(3x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39a1c600-8d35-5671-bcb1-62682d750cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110489 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{2}⁡3+{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =k+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110696 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110696 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921861109